o you have a policy for post-job inspections?” A company that employs a quality control manager or requires a supervisor to sign off on every job has built a system that counteracts the individual tendency to rush. You’re not hiring a person; you’re hiring their process. 2. **Use a “test job” approach.** Don’t hire for the big, messy repair first. Have them do something small and observable: replace a faucet, fix a running toilet. State your expectations upfront: “I’ve had bad experiences with plumbers rushing and not cleaning up. I’m looking for someone who will take their time and do a final walk-through with me.” If they roll their eyes or seem put off, you’ve just saved yourself the bigger nightmare. If they say “Absolutely, that’s how I work,” and then deliver, you’ve found someone to handle the major projects. 3. **Offer to pay for the estimate.** This flips the dynamic. Most residential customers expect free estimates, which forces plumbers to treat the visit as a sales call, not a diagnostic. Say, “I want a detailed, written assessment and a fixed price for the repair, and I’ll pay you for an hour of your time to do that properly.” You immediately signal you value their expertise, not their speed. The unmotivated ones will often decline because they don’t want to be held to that standard. 4. **Look for a “Master Plumber” designation specifically.** A Master Plumber has years more training and licensing than a Journeyman. They’ve invested significantly in their career and have a credential to protect. When you call, ask, “Will the person doing the actual work be a Master Plumber, and can I see their card?” This filters out a lot of volume-chasers. I know this might sound like you’re the one doing all the extra work just to get basic competence—and that’s a fair point. It shouldn’t be this hard. But rather than trying to convince yourself that “not all plumbers” are a certain way, you can change the selection funnel so the ones who survive it are, by definition, not the ones you’ve been getting. You don’t need to change the whole group; you just need to connect with the small subset that doesn’t fit your painful pattern.
